<div class="page">
  <h2>FROM THE EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR</h2>

  <p>I had intended this column to be about philanthropy and alumni relations, but a lot has happened since our last publication. Plans change. People change. Institutions change. We have been so fortunate to have such a dedicated group of alumni who continue to support the School of Dentistry. Mike Reddy, would be retiring and assuming the role of <span data-field="title">{{ title }}</span>. Mike has been an invaluable member of our community for many years, and I look forward to his continued involvement with the School of Dentistry for nearly thirty years. I would like to reflect back on the past few years and maybe take a peek toward the future.</p>

  <p>First and foremost, Mike Reddy was the best boss I ever had, and I've had plenty in my thirty years of working in higher education. He was able to maintain a high level of passion and effectiveness during his entire tenure with UAB Dentistry. In all things he did, Mike wanted the School to be the very best. From day one, he was strategic and inclusive. In his first year, he set a vision for the School that was both ambitious and achievable. He built a team that was passionate and effective, and he has always empowered his leadership.</p>

  <p>In fundraising, I couldn't have asked for a better partner. Mike Reddy didn't always tell our donors what they wanted to hear. He spoke with vision and optimism. I remember the first major prospective donor we visited early in his tenure. Mike painted a picture of how we wanted to achieve clinical excellence and make the School the best in the world. We walked away with a $500,000 commitment during our seven year engagement. We have since surpassed that goal, and we continue to grow. We have seen the School grow and thrive, and we have seen our alumni give generously to support the School. We have seen the School grow and thrive, and we have seen our alumni give generously to support the School.</p>

  <p>Our travels weren't always glamorous, actually they rarely were. Once, while visiting a donor in Guntersville and driving to a dental meeting in Chattanooga, I had a flat tire. Unfortunately, it was 100 degrees outside, I had had knee surgery, and I was running behind schedule. What did our donor do? He changed the flat tire, and he had knee surgery, and he helped me get back on the road. He was a true gentleman, and he made my day. He was a true gentleman, and he made my day. He was a true gentleman, and he made my day.</p>

  <p>We have achieved so much during his tenure. Our Comprehensive Care Clinic renovation will soon be complete. That renovation, along with others, gives us a state-of-the-art facility that will serve our students and faculty for years to come. We have also expanded our clinical offerings and expanded our reach into the community. We have also expanded our reach into the community.</p>

  <p>No one knows what the future holds, but we can look to the past for an indication. I know our alumni, faculty and staff will do everything they can to ensure our future success. I know our alumni, faculty and staff will do everything they can to ensure our future success. I know our alumni, faculty and staff will do everything they can to ensure our future success.</p>

  <p>Thanks for all you do – please keep it up!</p>

  <p>Regards,</p>
  <p><span data-field="full_name">{{ full_name }}</span>, CFRE</p>
  <p><span data-field="title">{{ title }}</span>, UASOD Alumni Association</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2>DEVELOPMENT NEWS</h2>

  <h3>A Once in a Lifetime Experience</h3>
  <p>By <span data-field="full_name">{{ full_name }}</span>, CFRE</p>

  <p>It was unlike any train I'd ever ridden. There was no click-clack, click-clack. No bouncing or jostling or even a loud hum. We were going nearly 200 miles an hour and the ride was as smooth as falling on still waters. I looked across the aisle from me and saw Harrison Filler, a fourth year dental student, peering through the window of the Bullet Train pointing out Mt. Fuji to his classmates and hosts. I could hardly believe I was in Japan with UAB School of Dentistry students as part of our exchange program.</p>

  <p>The exchange program's coordinator, Dr. Steve Filler (yes, Harrison's dad), had asked me to co-chaperone a group of our students with Dr. Dan Givan. Of course, our students didn't need chaperoning as they're all adults, but I happily accepted. I had never been to Japan. Had never really given thought to it. But I knew that this would be a once in a lifetime experience for a group that would exchange cultural and educational ideas with our sister institutions in Japan.</p>

  <p>UAB Dentistry D4 students Hillary Diffie, Harrison Filler, Austin Grissom, Ashley Turner Lee, Kenneth Liu, Amanda Morris, Drew Owens, Zehra Syed, Danielle Williams, and Dan "There You Are" Wright had been selected to participate in the program. They had already hosted their Japanese counterparts last summer and this was their return experience. A once in a lifetime experience.</p>

  <p>"They immersed us in their culture. We ate their food. We met their friends," said Dr. Filler. "We learned about their history and their traditions. We learned about their culture and their way of life. We learned about their culture and their way of life."</p>

  <p>The exchange (March 30 thru April 5) included attending lectures and visiting the dental clinic at Asahi University. We were also invited to visit the University of Tokyo, which is one of the most prestigious universities in the world. We toured the Meikai University School of Dentistry and met with various faculty members. We also met with students from other universities in Japan. We were able to see how they treated patients, said Dr. Kenneth Liu. The Japanese students entered dental school after high school graduation. They have six years of dental school, and they are in the process of becoming licensed dentists. They have six years of dental school, and they are in the process of becoming licensed dentists.</p>

  <p>Experience generally comes in compulsory residencies after dental school. Hillary Diffie, a fourth year student from UAB, was fascinated by the Japanese dental system. She said, "It's so different from what we do here. We have a good night or a bad night depending on your exams. Exams are big there," she said.</p>

  <p>While the educational and clinical components were an important part of the trip, it was clear to me, the cultural side of the exchange made the biggest impression on our students. Our dining experiences were memorable. Our meals of sushi, squid, eel, miso soup, beef, pork, shrimp, dumplings, and shabu-shabu (a hotpot dish of thinly sliced meat and vegetables boiled in water) were exotic. "I'll never forget the food. Some I really liked," said Grissom. With a grin, a with a grin, a with a grin, he said.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2>DEVELOPMENT NEWS</h2>

  <h3>A Once in a Lifetime Experience Continued</h3>

  <p>Our hosts from Meikai (Sone) treated us to a full day at Tokyo Disneyland. Much like its Disney counterpart in the states, the park was teaming with families. It was crowded because of the Japanese spring break, but our students made the most of their visit. Another highlight was a banquet aboard a traditional Japanese ship, cruising the Tokyo Bay. We were treated to a traditional Japanese dinner, which included sushi, eel, and other delicacies. We were also treated to a traditional Japanese dinner, which included sushi, eel, and other delicacies.</p>

  <p>On our last night there, our group gathered for dinner to discuss their experiences and share the most memorable impressions. They talked about touring the universities, dining, sightseeing, and karaoke. To my great pleasure, all of our students were able to share their thoughts and feelings about the trip. They were all so excited about the cultural differences we developed. They were so generous and accommodating. We were treated like royalty and I'm sure that filters down to their dental professional.</p>

  <p>It's think it's important for our students, faculty and alumni to know how professional, cordial, and impressive our dental students are. They are ambassadors for the UAB School of Dentistry, and they represent the best of our institution.</p>
  
  <h3>Alumnus Paul Weeks "Honored to Give" $500,000</h3>

  <p>Dr. Paul R. Weeks, Sr. has taken the first step to endow a professorship to benefit the UAB School of Dentistry's Department of Periodontology. The Huntsville, Ala. periodontist is making a total gift of $500,000 that includes current giving and estate giving. Weeks graduated from the UAB School of Dentistry in <span data-field="year">{{ year }}</span> and completed the periodontics post-graduate program at UAB in <span data-field="year">{{ year }}</span>. He said, "I've always been a part of the UAB community, and I'm so proud of what we've accomplished here."</p>

  <p>"My two years residency gave me the valuable skills I needed to start my own practice and to contribute to the field of periodontology. I'm so grateful for the education I received at UAB, and I'm so proud to be a part of this community," he said.</p>

  <p>Dr. Weeks has provided leadership to several dental organizations during his 38-year career. He served on the UAB Dentistry Leadership Council, the school's fundraising and advisory board. Through his service on the Leadership Council and previous service as a part-time clinical instructor at the School of Dentistry, he has been a dedicated supporter of the School. He has been an excellent education which has enabled me to provide optimal care to all my patients. I look back and keep our school and community in my heart. I'm so proud to be a part of the UAB community."</p>

  <p>He has been involved with prior fundraising campaigns to help the school, including the Weatherford-Palancus Endowed Professorship in Periodontology. He said, "I hope my gift will help the next generation of students at UAB have the same opportunities that I had, and make a gift. They might also consider leaving something from their estate," he said.</p>

  <p>Dr. Weeks has worked hard for nearly four decades. Weeks still finds time to enjoy life outside of dentistry. He enjoys traveling with his wife, Alicia, and enjoys cycling, fishing, hiking and snow skiing.</p>

  <p>When asked to reflect on his time as a dental student and resident, Weeks admitted, "It wasn't all roses and it wasn't easy," but he says he's honored to give back to UAB and give future students and residents the same opportunity he had. He wants others from his generation to know that the school doesn't receive the state funding it once did. "Hopefully others will follow in my footsteps and give back. It is an honor to give to my school."</p>

